          %h2#references References
          %p
            Originally the String-group was just known by its generic name: with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} O \langle 8 \rangle
            being the topologist’s notation for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected"} 7-connected
            cover of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}O
            of the group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
          %p
            When it was realized that lifts of the structure maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathcal{B}O
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            through the projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}O\langle 8 \rangle \to \mathcal{B}O
            – now called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+structure"} String structure
            – play the same role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            does in ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %em String group
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega (\mathcal{B}O\langle 8 \rangle)
            was suggested.
          %p
            Following some inquiries by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
            and confirmed in private email by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Haynes Miller
            it seems that the first one to propose the term
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi String
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} String
              group
            for the group known to topologists as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega (\mathcal{B}O\langle 8\rangle)
            was
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Haynes Miller
          %p
            A model of the string group by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets"} local nets
            of fermions is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Stolz"}> Stefan Stolz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
              %em The spinor bundle on loop space
              (2005) (
              %a{:href => "http://web.me.com/teichner/Math/Surveys_files/MPI.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Many more models exist by now in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            of a model for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+2-group"} string 2-group
          %p A good review is in the introduction of
          %ul#Schommer-Pries
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
              ,
              %em Central Extensions of Smooth 2-Groups and a Finite-Dimensional String 2-Group
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.2483"}> arXiv:0911.2483
              )
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#NikolausSachseWockel
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"}> Thomas Nikolaus
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Sachse"} Christoph Sachse
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Wockel"} Christoph Wockel
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Smooth Model for the String Group
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rns154"} 10.1093/imrn/rns154
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1104.4288"} arXiv:1104.4288
          %p
            it is shown that the topological string group does admit a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frechet+manifold"} Frechet manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            structure.
          %p
            For relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+nets"} conformal nets
            see
          %ul
            %li
              Bas Janssens,
              %em Notes on Defects &amp; String Group
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.bjadres.nl/WorkInProgress/Defect&String.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
